INT. MERIDIAN LOGISTICS - BREAK ROOM - DAY

A kitchenette with a laminate table and a crooked sign: "YOUR MOTHER DOESN'T WORK HERE." A sheet cake sits on the table, half-eaten, the words "CONGRATULATIONS" down to "CONGRATU."

BARTHOLOMEW GRIGG (50s, middle manager, tie knotted too tight) hovers near the cake. FARHAD ELYASI (30s, sales, phone in hand) scrolls. LEOPOLDO VANTERPOOL (40s, accounts, cardigan) refills his mug. ADEWALE BROOKMAN (30s, IT, lanyard heavy with badges) leans on the fridge.

                    BARTHOLOMEW
          So the cake is for Farhad.

                    FARHAD
          It's for the quarter.

                    LEOPOLDO
          It's for the department.

                    ADEWALE
          It's for whoever's still here.

                    BARTHOLOMEW
          He gets the first slice.

                    LEOPOLDO
          Who?

                    BARTHOLOMEW
          He does.

                    FARHAD
          I already had one.

                    LEOPOLDO
          Who's "he"?

                    ADEWALE
          He is Farhad. Obviously.

                    BARTHOLOMEW
          Not "he," Farhad. I meant Leopoldo.

                    LEOPOLDO
          I don't want cake.

                    FARHAD
          Then why is he holding a fork?

                    LEOPOLDO
          Who?

                    ADEWALE
          Please stop saying "he."

                    BARTHOLOMEW
          I'll cut it myself.

He grabs the knife. It slips. The cake splits down the middle. "CONGRATU" becomes "CON" and "GRATU."

                    FARHAD
          Perfect.
